// txt_ui -- how many screen pixels one font pixel becomes.
//
// The surface is the window now (ARCHITECTURE.md §3), so a fixed-size font is a
// *shrinking fraction* of the screen as the window grows: the same text that
// filled a line at 640x400 is a quarter of the height of a 1280x800 one. Holding
// the ratio of text size to window size constant is what this answers.
//
// **The step is an integer, and that is not a compromise made lightly.** The
// glyphs are a bitmap and txt_px_sc draws each font pixel as an n by n rect. A
// fractional magnification would mean resampling a bitmap font, which on eight
// pixels of glyph width is not a smaller version of the letter -- it is a
// different, worse letter, with rows dropped unevenly. Integer steps keep every
// stroke exactly one pixel wide and every glyph identical to its neighbours.
//
// This answers a *magnification*, not a size, so the move from an 8x8 face to an
// 8x16 one did not touch it: text got taller everywhere at once, which is what
// the change was for. What it does mean is that one line of UI chrome is now 16
// pixels at step 1 rather than 8, and anything that reserved a row of exactly 8
// had to be found -- grep for txt_cellh() to see where.
//
// 640x400 is the reference: the size the editor was laid out at, and the size the
// engine's measured budget is quoted for. The scale is rounded to nearest rather
// than floored -- adding half the reference before dividing -- so a 960-wide
// window reads as 2 rather than clinging to 1 until it doubles.
//
// The smaller of the two axes wins, so text never grows past what a short window
// can show; a wide, shallow window is the case that would otherwise overflow.

txt_ui() {
  int step_w = txt_step((import sf_gw), 640);
  int step_h = txt_step((import sf_gh), 400);
  int ui = txt_ui_min(step_w, step_h);
} return int ui;

// Round-to-nearest division: (n + ref/2) / ref.
txt_step(int n, int ref) {
  int step = (n + ref / 2) / ref;
} return int step;

// The screen extent of font pixel i at a per-mille scale: the distance between
// its two rounded boundaries. Consecutive pixels therefore tile exactly, with no
// gap and no overlap, and the total is exact however the individual widths land.
// Below a scale of 1000 some of them are zero, which is what shrinking a bitmap
// font means -- strokes drop out rather than the glyph turning to mud.
txt_ext(int i, int sc) {
  int ext = (i + 1) * sc / 1000 - i * sc / 1000;
} return int ext;
